#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048418 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999 :topic/math-academy-id 4139 :topic/title "Differentiating Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488 :topic/math-academy-id 207 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Pythagorean Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860 :topic/math-academy-id 997 :topic/title "The Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :topic/id #uuid "9d4da633-779f-5c01-bae4-e1784b64c369" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119559 :question/id #uuid "98c98a56-ca57-5dc6-9dc4-c40da53806f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3063" :question/problem "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve  $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$  to $t = 2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/57/574a16e843a9c81e5fc9ddbbe2c0dc2c308ffcac4991bcbf48c7f45b4871c93b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119560 :question/id #uuid "8932c22b-465f-5a48-962c-e53730e5ba40" :question/math-academy-id "e-3064" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf g (t) = \\langle 3 - 2 \\sin 2t, \\: 1 - 2\\cos 2t \\rangle$ from $t=\\dfrac \\pi 3$ to $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/29/29c97e5963d451cc389944ef113764b2d15998448e773a02bb98c062207c2860.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡2t+16{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119675 :question/id #uuid "118ad24e-627f-51ff-9b9c-9319c50956f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6303" :question/problem "![](images/85/8573aedcd26cba038b2db072560cd3d6ba7676542888a2d9b8a356713eaf6724.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t ,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom  $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1837 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050289 :topic/math-academy-id 2107 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051045 :topic/math-academy-id 1838 :topic/title "Parameterization by Arc Length"}] :topic/title "The Arc Length of a Vector-Valued Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048423 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048157 :topic/math-academy-id 394 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations Using a Difference of Squares"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832 :topic/math-academy-id 1422 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations with Leading Coefficients by Factoring"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050497 :topic/math-academy-id 393 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations with No Constant Term"}] :topic/id #uuid "9ddcc703-a984-5097-a142-236dc3c2fbaf" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157170 :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/math-academy-id "e-7836" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1}  = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x-1}-2x{e}^{x-1}-15{e}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-15){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15  = 0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157171 :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7838" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1}-16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-16){3}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3},$ and their product is $\\left( -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2979 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050617 :topic/math-academy-id 2859 :topic/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Exponential Functions and Polynomials"}] :topic/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742 :topic/math-academy-id 1049 :topic/title "Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :topic/id #uuid "a01c36d3-5643-5685-845e-29c37e89238f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115070 :question/id #uuid "dc6dd9b4-de7f-52d0-8ff9-a7a7f7a9abd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4711" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/40/406033f059dc4438ea55d43d2bd00b3b79bb99365a2e4846c995f4a447acfd82.svg)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/be/bed2784d17ee63b805917b492052daa7ef079c1682bbc78468eb10f314a92b1f.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/91/91175ab97472067cd14ffff740bb3a98718436f1e94de7a4a6d7fdfb1b35b81f.svg)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115071 :question/id #uuid "f2c7cfd3-da43-5208-bd0e-21692357fabe" :question/math-academy-id "e-4712" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/6f/6fa4508463b059ce8ef9a5cfa720de9463e41ecdbfb16f4460870bb6fce444bc.svg)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](images/21/215ff061dc7584055bf607f48d0048cb6bde4dcb1ed028fbf4c3a4bd47d89c25.svg)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115124 :question/id #uuid "cd56314d-f45a-55be-9762-983fe507a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6173" :question/problem "Given the system\n![](images/0e/0e89fc57b6af2ddf2a71246dbadc7feb8425d1e1e0ab8f988e7e8ad97d22cff7.svg)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/3e/3e3e8adb0d6e13f7d3b4c3e450b25a789480ac53189ab92385174688e460b2cc.svg)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/41/4122fb4ef56aa99161b15bc25001ca728a6cf7dae36d5dac0aac9b03dc411685.svg)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1733 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181 :topic/math-academy-id 1854 :topic/title "The Null Space of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315 :topic/math-academy-id 1852 :topic/title "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"}] :topic/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795 :topic/math-academy-id 3684 :topic/title "Further Solving of Two-Variable Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172038 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171844 :question/id #uuid "231de5e4-8c55-5129-baaf-57673050ddbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8505" :question/problem "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/0c/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171845 :question/id #uuid "0444de80-19f1-5251-b159-5dc55756bb19" :question/math-academy-id "e-8506" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = -2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171846 :question/id #uuid "3602a14d-6bd7-5586-a6e8-51d069dc8641" :question/math-academy-id "e-8507" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171847 :question/id #uuid "ab9fe3df-7f60-55c5-b8af-810b0746993e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8576" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x = -1$ or $y = -1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/94/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048885 :topic/math-academy-id 3350 :topic/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}] :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}
